  "notes": "The other founding future-house producer, and the one who pushed it furthest toward radio pop; the defining window is 2014-2020 inside the stated 1995-present career. The tempo is documented as a default rather than a band — \"126 is his default (catalogue readings cluster hard on 126, with 120-130 as the outer band)\" — so the mode is 126 across a 120-130 range. His signature move is authored in the melody and the countermelody together: \"a pitched vocal chop of the lead singer's own line used as the drop's instrumental hook, doubled by a plucked or muted-saw lead\", which is a chopped-vocal timbre with an octave-echo counter at a high density. The bass is the future-house one — \"elastic, FM-modulated, pitch-bent into each note, filter-swept, hard sidechained — carrying the drop melody in the low-mids\" — a 0.55 root probability with a 0.38 glide. The kit is \"a tight four-square kick with a short tail, clap/snap on 2&4, 16th hats with the open hat on the offbeat, and a snare-roll-plus-riser build shorter than the big-room norm — the drop arrives faster because the vocal has already done the tension work\", which is why the prechorus is 4 bars against Hardwell's and Garrix's 8 and 16. Harmony is \"pop-functional more often than in the rest of the future-house field because the records are written around sung verses\" `[LOW-CONF]`, a mark repeated here. Loops are 8 bars. ⚠ The drum, bass and chop grammar is convention-derived by the entry's own statement; the velocities and densities are INFERRED.",
